<TMU|<tuple|1.1.0|2026.2.5>>

<style|<tuple|generic|table-captions-above|number-europe|preview-ref|smart-ref|reduced-margins|doc-tmu>>

<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|Mogan STEM: A Low-Entropy Structured Representation <with|font-series|bold|Empowering> AI for Science>>

  <abstract-data|<abstract|As large language models (LLMs) increasingly assist scientific writing, limitations and the significant token cost of <TeX> generating become more and more visible. This paper analyzes <TeX>'s fundamental defects in lack of structural representation to illustrate its limitations on generated semantics and error localization. As an alternative, <with|font-series|bold|Mogan STEM>, a low-entropy structured representation, is introduced. Mogan outperforms <TeX> in the above aspects by its efficient data structure and clear contextual boundaries. Extensive experiments are conducted to verify Mogan's superior performance in LLM tasks. Furthermore, we show that due to Mogan's lower information entropy, it is more efficient to use <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> (the document format of Mogan) to fine-tune LLMs than <TeX>, achieving a 43% reduction in training loss<rsup|>. Our work provides a more scalable and AI-friendly data representation in the era of AI4Science.>>

  <section|Legacy <TeX>: Architectural and Ecosystem Bottlenecks>

  <subsection|Introduction of <TeX>>

  For decades, <LaTeX> has been the standard for scientific writing <cite|lamport1994document>. However, it is not friendly to Large Language Models (LLMs). As an unstructured macro language <cite|van_der_hoeven_jolly_2020>, <LaTeX> brings significant challenges to modern AI-driven workflows <with|font-shape|italic|(we defer a brief history of <TeX> to <smart-ref|app:1>)>.

  <subsection|Principles of <TeX> Compilation>

  <TeX> uses a batch-processing model. It reads the source code from beginning to end and replaces macros step by step. It does not build an explicit document tree (Abstract Syntax Tree) in memory <cite|van_der_hoeven_gnu_2001>. Because it lacks internal states, <LaTeX> must rely on multiple compilation passes to resolve cross-references and bibliographies. It writes temporary data into intermediate files (like <code*|.aux>, <code*|.toc>, and <code*|.bbl>), and reads them back in the next run <with|font-shape|italic|(detailed in <smart-ref|app:2>)>. This multi-pass design is inherently slow and opaque. It prevents the sub-second, real-time feedback required by modern editors and AI agents <cite|madje2023typst>.

  <subsection|Architectural Defects in <TeX>>

  <TeX> processes documents as a linear sequence of commands. Structural semantics and typesetting decisions are deeply coupled within a single execution path. Consequently, AI models and static analysis tools cannot reliably extract the document's structure without actually executing the code <with|font-shape|italic|(see <smart-ref|app:3> for a deep analysis of these defects)>. This design also leads to delayed and ambiguous feedback in error handling. The reported location of an error is rarely where the mistake actually happened. A subtle local defect can cause an irreversible interruption of the entire compilation process. Insufficient error logs provide no structural guidance, leaving models trapped in infinite debugging loops and hallucinations <cite|jain2026bibby>. This mechanism fundamentally constrains LLM-writing.

  <subsection|UX & Ecosystem Constraints>

  As detailed in <smart-ref|app:4>, <LaTeX>'s ecosystem is too heavy. A full TeX Live distribution usually exceeds 6GB. This huge size makes it difficult to deploy in cloud environments or sandboxes. Moreover, the ecosystem is highly fragmented. Different engines (e.g., pdfLaTeX, XeLaTeX, and LuaLaTeX) handle fonts and Unicode differently, causing inconsistent compilation results. Today, the continued dominance of <LaTeX> relies more on user habits and publisher rules rather than technical advantages <cite|van_der_hoeven_jolly_2020>. It is time to adopt a more scalable, AI-friendly data representation.

  <paragraph|Related work> The challenges of using <LaTeX> in LLM-driven workflows have been documented from multiple angles. On the generation side, benchmarks reveal a consistent pattern: LLMs struggle with <LaTeX>'s long-range syntactic dependencies and implicit semantic conventions. The TeXpert benchmark<nbsp><cite|kale2025texpert> reports only 15% accuracy on complex <LaTeX> tasks, with logical errors—not superficial typos—accounting for 54% of failures, demonstrating that even state-of-the-art LLMs struggle with <LaTeX> code generation. Similar difficulties arise in modality-crossing tasks: Ling et al.<nbsp><cite|ling_table2latex_2025> found that reinforcement-learning-based table-to-<LaTeX> conversion suffers from persistent token inefficiency rooted in <LaTeX>'s verbose syntax, while Xia et al.<nbsp><cite|xia2024docgenome> performed large-scale benchmarks revealing that Equation-to-<LaTeX> and Table-to-<LaTeX> conversion tasks remain challenging even with extensive training data (Edit Distance <math|\<gtr\>0.21>), attributing these high edit distances to the information entropy of <LaTeX> source code. These generation-level failures become systemic when <LaTeX> is embedded in end-to-end scientific pipelines: Lu et al.<nbsp><cite|lu2024aiscientist> demonstrated that in a fully automated discovery framework (The AI Scientist), the <LaTeX> writing stage is a critical bottleneck—the model cannot perceive the rendered PDF, producing overflowing tables and placeholder text that require manual correction. Jain et al.<nbsp><cite|jain2026bibby> arrived at a complementary conclusion from the editor side: their Bibby AI system found that raw compiler logs are insufficient for error localization and proposed combining logs with a live AST, effectively acknowledging that <LaTeX>'s batch feedback model must be augmented with structural representations to support reliable AI assistance. Lyn and Graham<nbsp><cite|lyn2025translatex> further identify an “execution illusion” where LLMs produce linguistically fluent but unexecutable <LaTeX> code for scientific formatting, introducing TransLaTeX, a reasoning-and-control framework with compiler-level verification. Our work extends this line of reasoning by arguing that such augmentation is inherently limited—a document format designed around explicit tree structure, rather than one retrofitted with AST recovery, can more fundamentally resolve the feedback and semantic transparency problems these systems encounter.

  The representation efficiency of <LaTeX> also poses challenges upstream, during data preparation and model training. Paster et al.<nbsp><cite|paster2023openwebmath> documented the substantial engineering effort required to extract and normalize 14.7B tokens of mathematical content from web sources containing heterogeneous <LaTeX> fragments, attaching particular importance to mathematical content for LLM training. Lin et al.<nbsp><cite|lin2024accurate> showed that direct LLM processing of documents incurs prohibitive costs, achieving <math|30\<times\>> reduction only through semantic hierarchical indexing—a workaround necessitated by the verbosity of the underlying format, highlighting the need for more efficient document representations. That markup structure matters for model learning has been demonstrated more directly: Li et al.<nbsp><cite|li2022markuplm> showed with MarkupLM that jointly modeling text and markup tags improves document understanding, while Taylor et al.<nbsp><cite|taylor2022galactica> found in training Galactica that <LaTeX> equations are a first-order component of scientific language whose representational overhead affects model behavior. Taken together, these findings suggest that <LaTeX>'s syntactic redundancy—where equivalent renderings admit multiple source forms (e.g., <verbatim|\\frac{a}{b}> vs. <verbatim|{a \\over b}>)—reduces next-token predictability and inflates training costs. Evidence from neural code modeling further supports the importance of structured representations: TreeDiff<nbsp><cite|zeng2026treediffastguidedcodegeneration> demonstrated that AST-guided span masking significantly outperforms token-level random masking for diffusion-based code generation, particularly for longer sequences (36.59% vs. 33.54% pass@1 on HumanEval with 1024-token prompts), suggesting that hierarchical representations enable models to better capture long-range dependencies. In Section<nbsp><reference|sec:eff-in-sft>, we provide direct experimental evidence for this hypothesis by comparing fine-tuning convergence on <LaTeX> versus the lower-entropy <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> format, which provides a more information-dense and structurally explicit representation than linear <with|font-family|tt|.tex>, enabling more efficient learning.

  On the systems side, dissatisfaction with <TeX>'s batch compilation has produced two distinct architectural responses, neither of which fully addresses the problem our work targets. The first is incremental markup compilation: Mädje<nbsp><cite|madje2023typst> and Haug<nbsp><cite|haug2022typst> designed Typst with a functional type system and incremental compiler that achieves sub-second preview updates, resolving the latency problem but retaining a source-editing paradigm that still separates authoring from visual output. The second is interactive augmentation of existing <LaTeX> workflows: Gobert and Beaudouin-Lafon<nbsp><cite|gobert2022ilatex> proposed i-<LaTeX>, which overlays interactive “transitional” widgets onto a code editor to bridge source and rendered output—an approach that alleviates feedback delays without removing the underlying batch compilation dependency. Both strategies leave the core tension unresolved: the document's authoritative representation remains either linear markup (Typst) or <TeX> source (i-<LaTeX>), rather than an explicit semantic tree. The structured WYSIWYG approach pioneered by Van der Hoeven<nbsp><cite|van_der_hoeven_gnu_2001> with GNU <TeX>macs took a more radical position, demonstrating that high-quality mathematical typesetting can be achieved within a tree-based editor that maintains structure as its primary representation. Empirical evidence supports the case for such alternatives: Knauff and Nejasmic<nbsp><cite|knauff2014efficiency> showed in controlled experiments that <LaTeX> users can be slower and produce more formatting errors than Word users, while Tan and Rigger<nbsp><cite|tan2024inconsistencies> systematically documented visual inconsistencies across <TeX> engines and TeX Live versions, substantiating the fragility of the ecosystem under real-world version drift. Gardner et al.<nbsp><cite|gardner2025neuralatex> implement a deep learning library entirely in pure <LaTeX>; when the document is compiled, the <LaTeX> engine trains the network and generates figures, with their paper taking 48 hours to compile, illustrating the extreme runtime cost of <LaTeX> when used as a programmable substrate. Our work builds on the <TeX>macs lineage through Mogan STEM (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:mogan>), and contributes new evidence that the tree-structured <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> format not only improves editing responsiveness but also yields measurable advantages for LLM tasks—a dimension absent from prior structured-editor evaluations.

  <section|Mogan STEM: Structural WYSIWYG Architecture><label|sec:mogan>

  While <TeX> represents formulas through layout-oriented markup in the form of typesetting instructions, Mogan STEM adopts a tree-based, functional representation for both mathematical formulas and document structure. Moreover, Mogan STEM shows advantages over other editors in <smart-ref|tab:mogan-vs-other>.<nbsp><with|font-shape|italic|(A detailed introduction for Mogan STEM and comparison is provided in <smart-ref|app:5>.)><nbsp>The following discussion evaluates this mechanism with specific examples.

  <subsection|Tree-Structured Formulas and Document Structure><label|sec:tree-struc-on-mogan>

  Unlike <LaTeX>'s compilation model, which centers on linear text and macro expansion, TeXmacs features an explicit tree-based document structure designed from the outset. Document components—such as chapters, formulas, citations, and typesetting elements—exist as structured nodes, rather than being implicitly embedded within macro calls or token streams. This architectural distinction directly impacts citation updates, compilation efficiency, and the interactive user experience.

  A fraction serves as a primary example of this difference. In <TeX>, a fraction is represented as <verbatim|\\frac{1}{2}>; internally, the compiler processes <verbatim|\\frac>, <verbatim|{1}>, and <verbatim|{2}> serially. In Mogan STEM, however, the fraction is represented at the underlying layer as the Scheme code <verbatim|(frac "1" "2")>. This forms a tree structure, as illustrated in Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:frac-tree>.

  <big-figure|<tree|<verbatim|frac>|"1"|"2">|<label|fig:frac-tree>The Tree Structure of Mogan Formulas>

  Let us consider another example:

  <\example>
    <label|example:around-in-mogan> as the mathematical expression in Equation <smart-ref|eq:tree-struc>

    <\equation>
      <big|int><rsub|a><rsup|b>f<around*|(|x|)>\<mathd\>x=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|[|F<around*|(|x|)>|]>|\|><rsub|a><rsup|b><label|eq:tree-struc>
    </equation>

    Its Scheme representation<footnote|In fact, the code written in Mogan's <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> files is XML-like formatted instead of Scheme. These two formats can be converted in Mogan without information loss. Note that AI is sensitive to XML syntax<nbsp><cite|sui2024table>. Therefore, the document of Mogan is conductive to the training and task performance of LLMs.> is shown in Listing<nbsp><reference|lst:scheme-integral>:\ 

    <\session|goldfish|default>
      <\unfolded-io>
        \<gtr\>\ 
      <|unfolded-io>
        `(math (concat (big "int") (rsub "a") (rsup "b") "f" (around* "(" "x" ")") "\<less\>mathd\<gtr\>x=" (around* "\<less\>nobracket\<gtr\>" (around* "[" (concat "F" (around* "(" "x" ")")) "]") "\|") (rsub "a") (rsup "b")))
      <|unfolded-io>
        <math|<big|int><rsub|a><rsup|b>f<around*|(|x|)>\<mathd\>x=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|[|F<around*|(|x|)>|]>|\|><rsub|a><rsup|b>>
      </unfolded-io>
    </session>

    Note that the segment <math|<around*|[|F<around|(|x|)>|]>\|> is managed via the tree structure illustrated in Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:tree-representation>. Consequently, even if a user omits a parenthesis or two, this omission does not negatively affect the rendering of the entire mathematical expression in Mogan.\ 

    \;

    <big-figure|<tree|<verbatim|around*>|"<verbatim|[>"|<tree|<verbatim|concat>|"<verbatim|F>"|<tree|<verbatim|around*>|"<verbatim|(>"|"<verbatim|x>"|"<verbatim|)>">>|"<verbatim|]>"|"<verbatim|\|>">|<label|fig:tree-representation>tree representation of <math|<around*|\<nobracket\>|<around*|[|F<around*|(|x|)>|]>|\|>>>
  </example>

  Unlike <TeX>, this tree structure exists at the rendering level, not the syntax level. This rendering-level tree structure offers two distinct advantages:\ 

  <\enumerate>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Local Scoping of Input Errors:>

    <\itemize>
      <item>Errors do not cause the entire document to fail rendering (a major drawback of <LaTeX>).

      <item>Local edits do not trigger global layout instability (a major drawback of MS Word).
    </itemize>

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Parallel Processing:> The CPU renders data in parallel rather than serially, which significantly accelerates rendering speed.
  </enumerate>

  Let's look at another example. For a two-line layout like Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:double-line>, the representation is:

  <\center>
    <\listing>
      <verbatim|(document "Mogan Logo" "(figure )" (itemize (document (concat (item) "WYSIWYG Writing") "")))>
    </listing>
  </center>

  <big-figure|<image|arXiv-submission/figure/double-line.png|0.2par|||>|<label|fig:double-line>Image insertion between lines>

  This corresponds to the tree structure shown in <smart-ref|figure:multiline-mogan>. As the figure demonstrates, in Mogan, each line of content and every image block maps to an independent leaf node within the tree. Consequently, modifying an image (e.g., resizing or replacing it) triggers a re-render of only that specific leaf node, without disrupting the layout of surrounding lines. This design effectively resolves the issue common in unstructured editors such as Word, where local modifications often lead to global layout chaos.

  <big-figure|<tree|<verbatim|document>|“Mogan Logo”|<tree|<verbatim|figure>|image>|“WYSIWYG Writing”>|<label|figure:multiline-mogan>Mogan's multi-line data structure.>

  <subsection|Functional Symbol Representation>

  In <TeX>, all mathematical symbols and fonts are represented as strings. However, in Mogan STEM, certain special symbols are defined via functions. For instance, the inner product <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>>, as shown in Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:braket>, is an anonymous function that automatically scales according to the symbols it contains. While this is similar to <verbatim|\\langle \\rangle> in <LaTeX>, the difference lies in the fact that, as a complete function, the left and right brackets always renders a unified pair rather than isolated characters. To obtain a single bracket, the user manually deletes the undesired character from the rendered pair.

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:braket><around*|\<langle\>|<big|int>|\<rangle\>><around|\<langle\>|f|\<rangle\>>
  </equation>

  <subsection|Dynamic System Optimization>

  Mogan STEM treats citation relationships as structural links directly embedded in the document tree. This mechanism avoids a full re-parsing of the document when updating. Furthermore, Mogan employs on-demand plugin loading to ensure that system complexity is dictated by document content ranther than a pre-configured feature set. This mechanism significantly reduce burden for deploying cloud enviroments or sandboxes.<nbsp><with|font-shape|italic|(Further details on reference rendering and plugin loading are available in <smart-ref|app:6> and <smart-ref|app:7>.)>

  <section|Numerical Experiments: Measuring performance in LLM tasks>

  To verify the benefits of using Mogan STEM compared to <LaTeX>, we designed and conducted experiments on fast compiling/rendering, LLM task performance, and fine-tuning.

  <subsection|Benchmark on compiling/rendering time>

  We benchmarked the compilation and rendering time of Mogan against <LaTeX> across documents of varying contents. The result demonstrates that Mogan STEM outperforms <LaTeX> on both full compilation and incremental updates. In real-time LLM-writing, Mogan's tree-structured documents will drastically reduce agents' time cost by its low feedback delays.<nbsp><with|font-shape|italic|(Experimental setups and detailed timing data are documented in <smart-ref|app:8>.)>

  <subsection|Performance in LLM tasks>

  We test using <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> to train LLMs instead of <with|font-family|tt|.tex>. The highly standardized grammar and structured tree-node tags in <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> files help the models locate targets faster, complete contexts properly, and debug ill-formed structures efficiently. The benefits are summarized along three dimensions: locating document structure, merging files with distinct doc-styles, and debugging ill-formed documents using error messages, which will be discussed in the rest of this subsection.

  <subsubsection|Locating document structure>

  To evaluate the LLM's grasp of document structure, we designed tests on 4 LLMs. Each test has 20 questions (attached in Appendix<nbsp><reference|app:20ques>) about the article's structure from <with|font-family|tt|arXiv:2502.17655>. For each answer, we take

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|s>=max <around*|(|0,<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|5-<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|T|1\<times\>10<rsup|4>>|\<rfloor\>>>|<cell|<text|, right answer>>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|<text|, wrong answer>>>>>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|T> is the token usage for the input, thinking, output, and MCP tools, <math|<big|sum>u<rsub|s>\<in\><around|[|0,100|]>>. The reason for using 10k tokens as a scale is that when the LLM had high confidence and located the structure accurately, it always consumed less than 10k tokens per question on our experimental material. Higher token usage means more loops of thinking and more uses of MCP tools, which represents lower efficiency and higher costs<nbsp><cite|ling_table2latex_2025>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<label|fig:reading><image|arXiv-submission/figure/reading.pdf|0.8par|0.433037par||>>|Test on locating document structure>

  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:reading> illustrates the results. Even if it is not a fair comparison as the LLMs have been trained inherently on <LaTeX> corpora, Mogan took the lead for most LLMs. The reason is that the file structure in Mogan has higher information density: references are updated and stored directly (e.g., <verbatim|\<less\>associate\|sec:tree-struc-on-mogan\|\<less\>tuple\|5.1\|13\<gtr\>\<gtr\>>, where <verbatim|sec:tree-struc-on-mogan> is the label name, <verbatim|5.1> is the section number, and <verbatim|13> is the page number) in the <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> file after incremental update. In contrast, in <LaTeX>, the LLM needs to scan the entire document to determine the actual displayed environment number after compilation. Thus, the LLM can locate the environment quickly in Mogan.

  <subsubsection|Merging files with distinct doc-styles><label|sec:doc-style>

  We define <em|doc-style> informally as the style of macro naming and command usage in a document. Documents may have distinct macro aliases, redefined macros, and more. For example, the theorem environments could be named <verbatim|theorem> in one but <verbatim|thm> in another. The same macro could have different meanings if <verbatim|\\norm> is defined as <verbatim|\\left\\lVert #1 \\right\\rVert> in one but <verbatim|\\mid #1 \\mid> in another. The same command could have different usage if <verbatim|\\R> is defined as <verbatim|\\mathbb{R}> in one but <verbatim|\\textcolor{red}{#1}> in another.

  To verify the benefit of generating precise structured and standard grammar for LLM writing, we asked the LLMs to complete two assignments.

  Assignment 1 is to generate two files: <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tex> and <with|font-family|tt|proofs.tex>. <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tex> is a large collection of mathematical theorems in a doc-style that includes many newly-defined macros, redefined commands, and packages. <with|font-family|tt|proofs.tex> is the collection of proofs of the theorems above but disordered and has a distinct doc-style. <with|font-family|tt|proofs.tex> even includes conflicting packages compared to <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tex>. The connection between these two files is the cross-references of each theorem and equation. The LLM should guarantee that <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tex> and <with|font-family|tt|proofs.tex> generated can be compiled successfully alone.

  Assignment 2 is to merge the two files generated by each LLM. The merged file should be written in the same doc-style as the leading file <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tex>. The LLM should guarantee that the merged file can be compiled successfully and the proofs are placed properly below their theorems according to cross-references.

  We use Mogan STEM to generate <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tmu> and <with|font-family|tt|proofs.tmu> directly from their <with|font-family|tt|.tex> versions; the task in Mogan is similar. The content in both Mogan and <LaTeX> is identical after rendering, which is guaranteed by the <LaTeX> importing engine built into Mogan.

  Assignment 1 has 1 task (generate two files). Assignment 2 has 4 tasks (merge two files generated by 4 LLMs). For each task, we take

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|m>=max <around*|(|0,<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|20-2\<times\>E<rsub|ref>-<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|T|1\<times\>10<rsup|4>>|\<rfloor\>>-E<rsub|sty>>|<cell|<text|, success on the first try>>>|<row|<cell|10-2\<times\>E<rsub|ref>-<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|T|1\<times\>10<rsup|4>>|\<rfloor\>>-E<rsub|sty>>|<cell|<text|, success on the second try>>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|<text|, fail within two tries>>>>>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|T> is the token usage for the input, thinking, output, and MCP tools, <math|E<rsub|ref>> is the number of reference failures (i.e., "??" appears but compilation succeeds), <math|E<rsub|sty>> is the number of cases where the merged file has the doc-style of <with|font-family|tt|proofs.tex> (we required LLMs to write the merged file in the doc-style of <with|font-family|tt|theorems.tex>, so other macro aliases or redefined macros are not accepted), <math|<big|sum>u<rsub|m>\<in\><around|[|0,100|]>>.

  As illustrated in Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:writing>, Mogan gains higher scores on all LLMs. LLMs struggle with <LaTeX> code generation on complex tasks<nbsp><cite|kale2025texpert>. The reason is that Mogan files have grammatical consistency so that the LLMs do not need to tackle conflicts and unify usage from two distinct doc-styles. The hallucinations and randomness from LLMs are strictly limited as well.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|arXiv-submission/figure/writing.pdf|0.8par|0.434053par||><label|fig:writing>>|Test on merging files with distinct doc-styles>

  Furthermore, merging contexts from two documents with distinct doc-styles is just a copy-and-paste task in Mogan STEM.

  <subsubsection|Debugging ill-formed documents using error messages>

  Debugging ill-formed documents is a common usage of LLM co-writing. We constructed several ill-formed documents (originating from <with|font-family|tt|arXiv:2502.17655>), fed the error messages to LLMs, and asked them to fix them. The test has 20 ill-formed samples as shown in Table<nbsp><reference|tab:ill-dist>.

  <big-table|<with|par-mode|center|<label|tab:ill-dist><tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|7|7|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<with|font-series|bold|Illness Type>>|<cell|<with|font-series|bold|Count>>>|<row|<cell|Unclosed bracket>|<cell|4>>|<row|<cell|Unclosed environment>|<cell|5>>|<row|<cell|Wrong command usage>|<cell|4>>|<row|<cell|Undefined cross-reference>|<cell|3>>|<row|<cell|Conflicting packages>|<cell|2>>|<row|<cell|Self-recursive macros>|<cell|2>>>>>>|Distribution of illness types in samples>

  For each illness, we take

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|d>=max <around*|(|0,<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|5-<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|T|1\<times\>10<rsup|4>>|\<rfloor\>>>|<cell|<text|, right answer>>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|<text|, wrong answer>>>>>>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|T> is the token usage for the input, thinking, output, and MCP tools, <math|<big|sum>u<rsub|d>\<in\><around|[|0,100|]>>, as illustrated in Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:debugging>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|arXiv-submission/figure/debugging.pdf|0.8par|0.434053par||><label|fig:debugging>>|Test on debugging ill-formed documents using error messages>

  The <LaTeX> group thinks for a long time to solve most of the error samples. The Mogan group locates the problems quickly and solves all of the error samples (only two samples consume more than 10k tokens). The reason is that the illness in <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> files only influences the closest tree-tag ancestor (as discussed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:tree-struc-on-mogan>). So the error message in Mogan STEM is quite clear for LLMs to understand and correct easily. In contrast, <LaTeX>'s error messages are usually detached from their root causes in large documents and the logs are very long.

  In fact, <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> files do not have problems like unclosed environments or self-recursive macros if they are written by Mogan STEM. In addition, Mogan STEM provides a WYSIWYG and intuitive user interface. If there is anything wrong in a <with|font-family|tt|.tmu> file, when opened by Mogan STEM, it is always clear to see where the problem is. And the rest of the document can be rendered correctly instead of being terminated in compilation like <LaTeX>.

  <subsection|Efficiency in fine-tuning><label|sec:eff-in-sft>

  Recall that Mogan uses a tree structure while <LaTeX> uses a linear macro flow. Benefiting from the tree structure, it is easier for models to predict the next token in Mogan than in <LaTeX>.

  We conducted a parallel supervised fine-tuning (SFT) experiment (machine configuration is shown in Appendix<nbsp><reference|app:ms>). We generated 1000 random formulas written in <LaTeX> and converted them to Mogan S-expressions<footnote|Also known as Scheme representations. It has been discussed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:tree-struc-on-mogan>.> using Mogan STEM. We guaranteed that the formulas in both Mogan and <LaTeX> are identical after rendering. The formulas cover fractions, radicals, subscripts and superscripts, matrices, piecewise functions, integrals and summations, limits, logical quantifiers, composite functions, and nested parentheses. Next, we cut the formulas into two parts. We gave the prefix part to the model and let it complete the rest.

  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:fine-tune> shows the experiment of low-rank adaptation (LoRA) based on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct<nbsp><cite|qwen2025qwen25> on 1000 formulas in 289 steps, following the approach of Dong et al.<nbsp><cite|dong2025machinelearninglm>. The Mogan group's loss converges to around 0.4 while <LaTeX>'s converges to around 0.7. Mogan achieves a 43% reduction in training loss<rsup|>. The reason is that Mogan's S-expressions have lower information entropy<nbsp><cite|xia2024docgenome>. <LaTeX> documents have a lot of syntax noise. For example, the code <verbatim|\\frac{a}{b}> and <verbatim|{a \\over b}> in <LaTeX> are equivalent after rendering; the code <verbatim|x^{2}> and <verbatim|x^2> are also equivalent after rendering. So the model has less certainty when predicting the next token in <LaTeX> compared to Mogan.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|arXiv-submission/figure/fine-tune.pdf|0.8par|0.495439par||><label|fig:fine-tune>>|Experiment of LoRA based on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct>

  Note that <LaTeX> documents have weaker grammatical consistency than Mogan. It is a burden for the model to predict the proper command in line with macro definitions in the preamble, especially in large documents written in several distinct doc-styles (discussed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:doc-style>) during training<nbsp><cite|lin2024accurate>.

  Moreover, discussions of Mogan versus Markdown are attached in Limitations.

  <new-page*><section*|Limitations>

  First, we did not fully compare Mogan STEM with Markdown in our experiments. Markdown is a lightweight language designed for daily notes. It lacks advanced typesetting features, such as customized templates and complex references. Given the huge gap in their application scenarios, designing fair comparative experiments is difficult.

  Second, Markdown uses the same <LaTeX> grammar for mathematical formulas. In <smart-ref|sec:eff-in-sft>, we showed that this <LaTeX> grammar is less efficient for LLM fine-tuning than Mogan's S-expressions. We believe this conclusion also holds for Markdown as a “Documentation-Oriented Architecture”<nbsp><cite|filho_doa_2026>. However, we did not empirically test this due to resource constraints.

  Finally, our current evaluation focuses mainly on LLM writing and training efficiency. We still need more experiments to evaluate other aspects, such as extensibility, semantic richness, and typographic precision. Due to computational and budget limits, we leave these large-scale evaluations for future work.

